WEBVTT

NOTE Module 04 transcript — auto-aligned to narration.wav (123.1s). en.

1
00:00:00.188 --> 00:00:03.008
Welcome to Module Four — advanced training.

2
00:00:03.417 --> 00:00:11.910
This is where you learn to optimize the way the papers do: better optimizers, learning-rate schedules, regularization, and fast data pipelines.

3
00:00:12.264 --> 00:00:14.586
Start with the optimizer zoo.

4
00:00:14.937 --> 00:00:19.196
For vision, plain SGD with momentum still beats Adam surprisingly often.

5
00:00:19.555 --> 00:00:27.075
Adam adapts a per-parameter learning rate for you, and AdamW fixes it by decoupling weight decay from the gradient step.

6
00:00:27.439 --> 00:00:34.052
But here's the thing — your learning-rate schedule usually matters more than which optimizer you pick.

7
00:00:34.418 --> 00:00:38.546
A constant learning rate plateaus early and wastes epochs.

8
00:00:38.902 --> 00:00:41.859
Step and exponential decay are simple but dated.

9
00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:51.516
The modern default is cosine annealing with a short warmup, and the OneCycle policy can give you super-convergence at a surprisingly large rate.

10
00:00:51.888 --> 00:00:56.516
Run an LR range test to find the highest stable value first.

11
00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:00.856
To fight overfitting, reach into the regularization toolkit.

12
00:01:01.217 --> 00:01:04.095
Dropout zeroes activations at training time only.

13
00:01:04.465 --> 00:01:08.459
Weight decay penalizes large weights, while L1 also sparsifies them.

14
00:01:08.808 --> 00:01:11.846
Label smoothing softens those hard one-hot targets.

15
00:01:12.203 --> 00:01:16.661
And early stopping simply remembers your best validation loss and restores it.

16
00:01:17.016 --> 00:01:18.433
Normalization deserves its own moment.

17
00:01:20.274 --> 00:01:25.569
Batch norm normalizes across the batch — great until your batch is tiny.

18
00:01:25.929 --> 00:01:30.208
Layer norm works per sample and is the default inside Transformers.

19
00:01:30.559 --> 00:01:36.405
Group norm is the batch-size-independent middle ground, and instance norm shows up in style transfer.

20
00:01:36.759 --> 00:01:39.340
Finally, make it fast.

21
00:01:39.724 --> 00:01:47.749
Mixed-precision FP16 buys roughly a one-and-a-half to two times speedup on modern GPUs, with a loss-scale optimizer guarding against underflow.

22
00:01:48.138 --> 00:01:58.618
Build the input side with tf.data — prefetch, cache, and AUTOTUNE — scale out with MirroredStrategy, and always profile before you guess where the bottleneck is.

23
00:01:59.993 --> 00:02:02.898
Let's benchmark optimizers and schedules — open the labs.
